<p>Planet Labs, a California-based satellite imaging company, told customers by email that it will indefinitely withhold public release of satellite images covering Iran and nearby conflict areas after a request from the U.S. government, according to multiple reports published on April 4-5. Reuters reported that the company will instead distribute the imagery on a restricted, case-by-case basis for selected uses, and that the policy applies retroactively to images dating back to March 9. The change limits access to commercial satellite views that journalists, researchers, and the public often use to monitor war zones when on-the-ground reporting is limited. That imagery has become an important way to document military damage and track developments in near real time.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/L6IKQ4KcxvlbassHMfFuXQu1QmN-AnXXqUoYcr1IoSdSO_i677ADhD1F5lXu8FFe-rwEk5wfJbt3RjNBGxltxbkdsiEZKJjvdL8Th_u1yPUcFgaWGOAXeDpY-SpndcpT3hGv_tnDPqEIAAOMG9rkFYyEqCqFoaHCVDg3f3MU-DDWrZllo4WwNSrA49sUF_s-9s7J2WD0YRqfJ1eeJZlYmQgA5aWIu6RV35cuEw2aAFsdhV7C3OQhRJZVt6Kr8l5xw_ZuiL3EbRKi4lIHO_Y8Nq_e0XY' alt='A satellite photo of Kharg Island in Iran in February that was distributed by Planet Labs.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Sector-wide request: Planet Labs said the U.S. government asked all satellite imagery providers to impose an indefinite withhold on images of the conflict region, suggesting this was broader pressure on the industry, not just a single-company decision.</li><li>Escalation of controls: Reuters said the indefinite hold followed earlier delays meant to limit potential military use of commercial imagery, pointing to a steady tightening of restrictions over time.</li><li>Image type: NDTV described the affected material as high-resolution aerial pictures, underscoring that the policy reaches the detailed commercial views often used for close visual analysis.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Planet Labs: The company told customers by email that it was imposi